# Created 2026-05-27 to enable manual `vercel --prod` deploys from repo root.
# The Vercel project has Root Directory = `frontend`, so it needs to resolve
# repo_root/frontend correctly — running from repo root works for resolution
# but uploads the entire repo (hits Vercel's 10mb upload limit).
# This file restricts the upload to ONLY the frontend/ subdirectory MINUS
# heavyweight artefacts (node_modules, dist, build logs).
*
!frontend
!frontend/**
# Vercel installs deps server-side; never upload node_modules (1.1GB on disk).
frontend/node_modules
# Build artefacts — Vercel re-runs `npm run build`; no need to upload.
frontend/dist
frontend/.next
frontend/build-output.log
# Test results + playwright artefacts
frontend/test-results
frontend/playwright-report
# Local env files (never upload secrets).
#
# A GLOB, NOT A LIST OF FILENAMES. This file opens with `*` and then re-includes all of
# frontend/ via `!frontend/**`, so last-match-wins means anything NOT named below is
# uploaded. Naming `.env` and `.env.local` specifically meant `.env.production`,
# `.env.production.local`, `.env.development.local` and `.env.staging` would each have been
# uploaded to the build — with their values — the moment one was created. Only `.env` and
# `.env.example` exist today, so nothing has been uploaded; this closes it before the next
# env file is added rather than after.
#
# Excluding `.env.example` too is deliberate and safe: nothing in the build reads any env
# FILE (Vercel injects environment variables from the project settings), and the only
# reference to `.env.example` anywhere in the build tree is a prose comment in
# frontend/scripts/verify-env.mjs, which the build never runs.
#
# Pinned by frontend/src/test/vercelUploadExcludesEnv.test.ts.
frontend/.env*
# Existing nested .vercel/ — the project link at repo root is canonical now
frontend/.vercel
